    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 12 and 20, we can use the Euclidean algorithm, which involves repeated division.\n\nFirst, divide 20 by 12:\n20 ÷ 12 = 1 with a remainder of 8\n\nNow, we have:\n12 = 12 × 1 + 8\n\nSince the remainder is 8, we replace 20 with 12 and 12 with the remainder:\n20 = 12 × 1 + 8\n\nNow, divide the next 12 by the new remainder:\n12 ÷ 8 = 1 with a remainder of 4\n\nReplace 12 with 8 and 8 with the new remainder:\n20 = 12 × 1 + 8\n8 = 12 × 1 + 4\n\nNow, divide the next 12 by the new remainder:\n8 ÷ 4 = 2 with a remainder of 0\n\nReplace 12 with 4 and 4 with the new remainder:\n20 = 12 × 1 + 8\n8 = 12 × 1 + 4\n4 = 8 × 1 + 0\n\nSince we have a remainder of 0, the GCD of 12 and 20 is the last non-zero remainder, which is 4. Thus, the greatest common divisor of 12 and 20 is \\boxed{4}.",
